// First Approach {O(N)}
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> getConcatenation(vector<int>& nums) {
        int nums_size = nums.size();
        vector<int> new_nums;
        for (int i = 0; i < nums_size; i++){
            new_nums.push_back(nums[i]);
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < nums_size; i++){
            new_nums.push_back(nums[i]);
        }
        return new_nums;
    }
};

- Although correct, this is the slowest approach due to dynamic memory overhead.
- `new_nums` starts with a size and capacity of 0.
- On every `push_back`, C++ vectors utilize a geometric growth strategy (doubling capacity when full: 0 -> 1 -> 2 -> 4 -> 8 -> 16...).
- Each capacity breach forces the system to allocate a new memory block, copy all existing elements, and deallocate the old block. 
- While the amortized time complexity remains O(N), these frequent background operations cause a noticeable performance penalty.

//Second Approach {O(N)}
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> getConcatenation(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<int> new_nums = nums;
        new_nums.insert (new_nums.end(), nums.begin(), nums.end());
        return new_nums;
    }
};

- Cleaner code, but it still triggers a single memory reallocation penalty.
- Initializing `new_nums = nums` sets its initial capacity to exactly N.
- When `std::insert` attempts to append the next N elements, the vector runs out of space and is forced to reallocate to a capacity of 2N. This results in the first N elements being copied twice.

//Third Approach {O(N)}
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> getConcatenation(vector<int>& nums) {
        int nums_size = nums.size();
        vector<int> new_nums(2 * nums_size);
        for (int i = 0; i < nums_size; i++){
            new_nums[i] = nums[i];
            new_nums[i + nums_size] = nums[i];
        }
        return new_nums;
    }
};

- Fastest Approach because new vector new_nums has a fixed size block of memory allocated.
- Instantly allocates a fixed memory block for all 2N elements right at the start.
- Eliminates all resizing, reallocation, and data shifting; the loop directly overwrites pre-allocated memory addresses via direct indexing.

//Fourth Approach {O(N)}
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> getConcatenation(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<int> new_nums;
        new_nums.reserve (2 * nums.size());
        new_nums.insert(new_nums.end(), nums.begin(), nums.end());
        new_nums.insert(new_nums.end(), nums.begin(), nums.end());
        return new_nums;
    }
};

- Ties for Fastest Approach.
- Uses `reserve()` to allocate a fixed block upfront (like Third Approach), but retains the clean, loopless syntax of built-in C++ functions.
